On 30 Mar 2010, at 18:53, Kevin Reid wrote:

> These seem to me to be the essential elements of the situation. (I do not=
 speak for Michael Everson, etc.)

I do. ;-)

> * Michael Everson wants to publish versions of Alice, including a Lojban =
version. These versions are all in Latin script, and he wants them to *look=
 nice*, and he considers traditional Latin typography-and-punctuation-etc. =
to be nice and the obvious way to do it.

Exactly so.

> * Standard Lojban has rules about the use of punctuation, which conflict =
with the above.

Yes, but it also has some options which allow for some choices. If acutes a=
re used for stress, caps are unused and are free for other uses. Same goes =
with dots. (The other uses are non-standard.)

> * Michael Everson wants to publish his Lojban Alice satisfying both his p=
references and ours. Since there is a conflict, the discussion of the parti=
cular points will hopefully help determine a compromise which is not *UNNEC=
ESSARILY* offensive to either side.

It would be nice.=20

> Yes, Everson *can* publish without any input from us, but that doesn't me=
an he *should*. He's doing the polite thing and getting our input.

Thank you for saying so. I am pleased to see a fair bit of support, as well=
 as the middling support, and the grudging acknowledgement, and the out and=
 out rejection. What a diverse community!

> Even if the Lojban community doesn't accept his work as being adequate Lo=
jban, we at least know what his motives were. Even if Everson doesn't take =
our advice, he is at least more informed about the significance to Lojbanis=
ts of his choices; he can make choices which which are neutral to him and l=
ess irritating to us.

I guess I can make no promises. But I'm interested in possibilities.

> Of course, a large portion of the discussion is bikeshed painting, or wha=
tever you want to call it; but *stomping on the discussion is not the right=
 answer*.

What is this bike-shed thing? (I parsed this as bi-kesh'd the first time I =
saw it.)\

